      "subject_id": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000030",
      "chapter_id": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000041",
      "qtype": "short_answer",
      "marks": 3.0,
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "bloom": "understand",
      "language": "en",
      "stem": "Why are decomposition reactions called the opposite of combination reactions? Explain with an example.",
      "options": null,
      "answer": {
        "text": "Decomposition reactions are the opposite of combination reactions because in combination reactions two or more substances combine to form a single product, while in decomposition a single reactant breaks down into two or more products. For example, CaO + H₂O → Ca(OH)₂ is combination, while Ca(OH)₂ → CaO + H₂O is decomposition."
      },
      "rubric": {
        "key_points": [
          "Combination: two or more → one product",
          "Decomposition: one → two or more products",
          "Correct example of each"
        ]
      },
      "learning_outcome_ids": ["00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000051"],
      "competency_ids": [],
      "meta": {},
      "content_hash": "sha256:a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f60005",
      "status": "approved",
      "created_by": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000101",
      "prompt_version_id": null
    },

    {
      "_comment": "QT-6: Long Answer — Chapter 2",
      "id": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000406",
      "root_question_id": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000406",
      "previous_version_id": null,
      "version_number": 1,
      "is_current": true,
      "org_id": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000200",
      "scope": "org",
      "source": "textbook",
      "board_id": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001",
      "class_id": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010",
      "subject_id": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000030",
      "chapter_id": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000042",
      "qtype": "long_answer",
      "marks": 5.0,
      "difficulty": "hard",
      "bloom": "analyze",
      "language": "en",
      "stem": "Explain the process of preparation of bleaching powder. Write the chemical equation involved. List two uses of bleaching powder and explain why it loses its effectiveness when kept open for a long time.",
      "options": null,
      "answer": {
        "text": "Bleaching powder is prepared by the action of chlorine gas on dry slaked lime [Ca(OH)₂]. The chemical equation is: Ca(OH)₂ + Cl₂ → CaOCl₂ + H₂O. Uses: (1) For bleaching cotton, linen and wood pulp in textile and paper industries. (2) For disinfecting drinking water to make it free of germs. Bleaching powder loses its effectiveness when kept open because it reacts with atmospheric CO₂ and moisture, decomposing to release chlorine gas, which is the active bleaching agent."
      },
      "rubric": {
        "marking_scheme": [
          { "point": "Correct preparation method (dry slaked lime + Cl₂)", "marks": 1 },
          { "point": "Correct balanced chemical equation", "marks": 1 },
          { "point": "Two correct uses with brief explanation", "marks": 2 },
          { "point": "Explanation of loss of effectiveness (reaction with CO₂/moisture)", "marks": 1 }
        ]
      },
      "learning_outcome_ids": ["00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000055"],
      "competency_ids": [],
      "meta": {},
      "content_hash": "sha256:a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f60006",
      "status": "approved",
      "created_by": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000101",
      "prompt_version_id": null
    },

