    {
      "_comment": "QT-8: Case Study — Chapter 3",
      "id": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000408",
      "root_question_id": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000408",
      "previous_version_id": null,
      "version_number": 1,
      "is_current": true,
      "org_id": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000200",
      "scope": "org",
      "source": "institute_bank",
      "board_id": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001",
      "class_id": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010",
      "subject_id": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000030",
      "chapter_id": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000043",
      "qtype": "case_study",
      "marks": 4.0,
      "difficulty": "hard",
      "bloom": "evaluate",
      "language": "en",
      "stem": "Read the following passage and answer the questions that follow:\n\nIron is the most widely used metal. But iron articles are damaged by rusting. Rusting of iron is an oxidation reaction. The iron reacts with oxygen in the presence of moisture (water) to form iron oxide (Fe₂O₃·xH₂O), which is reddish-brown in colour. Rusting causes enormous damage to buildings, bridges, ships and many other iron articles. Prevention of rusting is very important. Some common methods include painting, oiling, greasing, galvanising and alloying.",
      "options": {
        "sub_questions": [
          {
            "stem": "What is the chemical formula of rust?",
